     specification asks.  The TRANSPORT failure names the server, its port and the
     reason: "connection failed with 10.38.22.60:49: Transport endpoint is not
     connected".  The ACCOUNTING failure is reported on its own line, and it tells the
     START apart from the STOP: "error sending start (fd)" and "error sending stop
     (fd)", so a reader can tell which record was lost.  The login itself still
     succeeded, so an accounting failure does not deny access, and that is correct.
     Measured side effect: the failed accounting attempt costs 3.1 s of extra login
