STEP 5 (designed) -- S1599.1.081: a log when communication is LOST                 PARTIAL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: this needs a server that WAS reachable and then is not.  That is hard on this
  bench.  The only working server is the box itself, directly attached on vlan10.
  Stopping tac_plus is the Test Engineer's to do, and shutting port1.0.10 or port1.0.48
  was forbidden for this run.  Two methods were tried.

  Method A -- a blackhole route.  It does NOT work, and that is worth recording.

awplus(config)# ip route 10.38.22.33/32 null

awplus# show ip route 10.38.22.33
  Routing entry for 10.38.22.33/32
    Known via "static", distance 1, metric 0
      is a summary, null

  The route installs, and the routing table shows it as the blackhole.  Yet the DUT
  kept reaching the server.  A console login straight afterwards still delivered its
  accounting records, task_id=50 and task_id=51, and "show tacacs+" still read Alive.
  A /32 blackhole does NOT override on-link delivery to a directly connected host for
  the DUT's own control-plane traffic.  The route was removed again with
  "no ip route 10.38.22.33/32 null".

  Method B -- a bogus static ARP entry.  This works.

awplus(config)# arp 10.38.22.33 0000.dead.beef

  State BEFORE, with communication established: "show tacacs+" read
  "10.38.22.33  Alive", a console login took 0.4 s, and accounting records were
  arriving with task_id=48 and task_id=49.

  State AFTER, driven with a console login.  The console is not affected by the broken
  ARP entry, while SSH from the box is, which is why this leg used the console:

  LOGIN RESULT: USER  user=manager  seconds=5.4            (0.4 s before)
  FILEWIN /var/log/tac_plus.acct ... new-bytes=0           (the server received nothing)

awplus# show tacacs+
  10.38.22.33                                   Dead       <-- was Alive

  2026 Oct 20 01:45:55 authpriv.warning awplus login: _pam_account: error sending stop (fd)
  2026 Oct 20 01:46:02 authpriv.warning awplus login: _pam_account: error sending start (fd)

  The entry was then removed with "no arp 10.38.22.33".  Note that the negative form
  takes NO MAC address: "no arp 10.38.22.33 0000.dead.beef" is rejected with
  "% Invalid input detected".  Recovery was verified:

  LOGIN RESULT: USER  user=manager  seconds=0.4
  FILEWIN ... new-lines=4        (task_id 51 stop, task_id 52 start and stop -- records
                                  are flowing again)
awplus# show tacacs+
  10.38.22.33                                   Alive

  => STEP 5 PARTIAL: the loss of communication is detected and recorded, but not as a
     log message.
     What works: "show tacacs+" moved from Alive to Dead, so the DUT genuinely knows
     that communication was lost.
     What does not: no log message says so.  The only lines produced are
     "_pam_account: error sending start (fd)" and "... stop (fd)", which are the
     ACCOUNTING failure of S1599.1.086.  They are not a statement about the server.
     The "tac_connect_single_vrf: connection failed" line of STEP 4 is ABSENT here,
     because this failure is a TCP timeout and not an immediate connect error.  So the
     "communication lost" case is logged LESS than the "never established" case.
     And nothing at all is logged when communication comes back, which is FINDING 1
     again.

  FINDING 2 -- S1599.1.081 is satisfied only by a show command, not by a log message.
  The device detects loss of communication precisely enough to move a server from Alive
  to Dead, and emits no log line to that effect.  An operator watching syslog sees only
  that an accounting record could not be sent.  They cannot tell from the log that a
  server has gone away, and they get no notification at all when it returns.  Against
  S1599.1.081, and against S1599.1.080 for the return, with S1599.2.25 removing the
  debugger that might otherwise cover for it.

  An observation on the "Last login:" banner, measured during this step.  While the
  server was unreachable, one console login printed the banner line TWICE:

      awplus login: manager
      Last login: Tue Oct 20 01:45:50 UTC 2026 on ttyS0
      Last login: Tue Oct 20 01:45:57 UTC 2026 on ttyS0
      AlliedWare Plus (TM) 0.0.0 10/12/26 06:38:00

  One "login:" prompt, one username echo, TWO "Last login:" lines about 7 s apart, one
  banner.  A login outside that window printed exactly one:

      awplus login: manager
      Last login: Tue Oct 20 01:48:22 UTC 2026 on ttyS0
      AlliedWare Plus (TM) 0.0.0 10/12/26 06:38:00

  Measured honestly: across the window in which the server was unreachable there were
  4 logins and 6 "Last login:" lines, so 2 of the 4 doubled.  Every login before and
  after the window produced exactly one.  The gap of about 7 s matches the accounting
  timeout.  The link to the unreachable server is clear.  The mechanism is not
  isolated, and this case did not establish why 2 of 4 doubled rather than all 4.  This
  is stated as an observation, not as a rule.

STEP 6 (designed) -- S1599.1.082: a log when login authentication SUCCEEDS            PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: this is the first step that needs the authentication method list.  A way back
  into configuration mode was built and proven FIRST, before the method list was
  touched, because platforms/x960.md section 7E.1 says a server Reject is honoured and
  the local fallback is skipped.

awplus(config)# username tacuser privilege 15 password <local password>
conlogin --user tacuser --password <local password> --expect user ...   -> USER, then awplus#

  The LOCAL password of "tacuser" differs from its TACACS+ password.  That makes STEP 7
  a discriminator as well as a control.  "aaa authentication enable default local" was
  left alone, so "enable" always works.  Only then:

awplus(config)# aaa authentication login default group tacacs+ local

conlogin --user tacuser --password <server password> --expect user
    --restore-user tacuser --restore-password <server password>
  LOGIN RESULT: USER  user=tacuser  seconds=0.6  prompt='awplus>'

DUT log, from "show log | include tacplus|FAILED LOGIN|aaa-configure", IMISH echoes
removed:

  2026 Oct 20 01:50:14 authpriv.notice awplus login: PAM-tacplus: User tacuser is authenticated
  2026 Oct 20 01:50:14 user.notice     awplus IMISH[11579]: [SCRIPT]aaa-configure tacuser enable-mode 2 cmd-acct-priv 0 update-intval 0
  2026 Oct 20 01:50:16 authpriv.notice awplus login: PAM-tacplus: User tacuser is authenticated
  2026 Oct 20 01:50:16 user.notice     awplus IMISH[11624]: [SCRIPT]aaa-configure tacuser enable-mode 2 cmd-acct-priv 0 update-intval 0

  => STEP 6 PASS, S1599.1.082 satisfied: exactly one "PAM-tacplus: User <name> is
     authenticated" line per successful login.  Two logins gave two lines.  The line
     names the user, at facility and severity "authpriv.notice".
     A second line is worth keeping even though no specification asks for it.
     "[SCRIPT]aaa-configure <user> enable-mode <n> cmd-acct-priv <n> update-intval <n>"
     is written at every login and records the AAA profile the session was given.  It
     is a useful anchor for correlation: it names the user, it appears once per login,
     and "cmd-acct-priv 0" and "update-intval 0" state the command-accounting and
     periodic-update settings in force for that session.

STEP 7 (designed) -- S1599.1.083: a log when login authentication FAILS               PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: same configuration.  The same account offers the password that exists only in
  the LOCAL database.  Section 7E.1 says it will be refused, because the server answers
  Reject.

conlogin --user tacuser --password <local password> --expect fail
    --restore-user tacuser --restore-password <server password>
  LOGIN RESULT: FAIL  user=tacuser  seconds=1.6  prompt='Login incorrect'

DUT log:

  2026 Oct 20 01:51:10 authpriv.err    awplus login: PAM-tacplus: auth failed: 2
  2026 Oct 20 01:51:11 authpriv.notice awplus login: FAILED LOGIN SESSION FROM ttyS0 FOR tacuser, Permission denied
  2026 Oct 20 01:51:18 authpriv.notice awplus login: PAM-tacplus: User tacuser is authenticated   <-- the restore login

  => STEP 7 PASS, S1599.1.083 satisfied: a failed login produces TWO lines, and between
     them they carry everything an investigation needs.  The PROTOCOL cause is at
     "authpriv.err": "auth failed: 2", where 2 is the TACACS+ AUTHEN_STATUS_FAIL code.
     So the log tells a server Reject apart from a transport problem, which STEP 4
     shows as "connection failed".  The SESSION-LEVEL record is at "authpriv.notice":
     "FAILED LOGIN SESSION FROM ttyS0 FOR tacuser, Permission denied", naming the line
     and the user.  The restore login's success line right afterwards shows the two
     outcomes are unambiguous against each other in one stream.

STEP 8 -- the facility and severity inventory                                         done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: a log message is only useful if a syslog collector can be told how to select
  it.  This table lists every TACACS+ event this case produced, with the exact fields a
  collector filter has to match.

  event                            facility.severity  program            text
  -------------------------------  -----------------  -----------------  -------------
  login authentication succeeded   authpriv.notice    login              PAM-tacplus: User <u> is authenticated
  (console)
  login authentication failed,     authpriv.err       login              PAM-tacplus: auth failed: 2
  server Reject
  login refused, session level     authpriv.notice    login              FAILED LOGIN SESSION FROM <line> FOR <u>, Permission denied
  server unreachable, connect      auth.err           sshd-session[pid]  tac_connect_single_vrf: connection failed with <ip>:49: Transport endpoint is not connected
  fails at once
  accounting record could not be   auth.warning       sshd-session[pid]  _pam_account: error sending start|stop (fd)
  sent (SSH session)
  accounting record could not be   authpriv.warning   login              _pam_account: error sending start|stop (fd)
  sent (console)
  the AAA profile applied at       user.notice        IMISH[pid]         [SCRIPT]aaa-configure <u> enable-mode <n> cmd-acct-priv <n> update-intval <n>
  login

  Two practical observations:
  1. There is no single facility, no single severity and no single keyword that selects
     all TACACS+ events.  The same kind of event is "authpriv.*" from the console and
     "auth.*" from an SSH session.  The text prefix is "PAM-tacplus:" for some lines,
     "tac_connect_single_vrf:" for others and "_pam_account:" for accounting.  Severity
     ranges from "notice" to "err" for outcomes of the same kind.  A collector filter
     has to be written as a union of patterns.  This is worth saying out loud, because
     S1599.2.25 makes these messages the whole diagnostic surface.
  2. Severity is not a fault indicator here.  Every "auth.err" line in this case is the
     DUT correctly reporting a control the tester created on purpose.

  => STEP 8 done: the table above is the set of TACACS+ log events this case produced
     on this build, with the facility, severity, program and text of each.

